<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chickamauga Cherokee</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chickamauga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> were a band of Cherokee who, under the war leader Dragging Canoe, refused to accept land cessions to colonists and moved in 1777 to the Chickamauga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> area near modern Chattanooga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>. Allied with the British and later supplied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, they waged war on American settlers for nearly two decades. The fighting ended with treaties in 1794, after which they rejoined the wider Cherokee nation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Chickamauga were followers of the war chief Dragging Canoe from the Overhill Cherokee towns on the landward side of the Appalachian continental divide, close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a>. While most Cherokee on the Atlantic seaboard, surrounded by settlers, made peace with the Americans in 1776–1777 after the Second Cherokee War, Dragging Canoe accused the elderly chiefs of endangering the survival of their people and never met with whites to negotiate a treaty again.</p><p>In the winter of 1776–77 his followers moved down the Tennessee River and settled where the Great Indian Warpath crossed Chickamauga Creek. American settlers named the band after the town of Chickamauga there; Wilma Mankiller, a former Principal Chief of the Cherokee Nation, called them Chickamaugans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnees</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogees</a> and exiles from other nations joined them, as did escaped slaves and British loyalists. After militias destroyed their towns, Dragging Canoe sent his brother Little Owl to ask the Muscogee chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-mcgillivray">Alexander McGillivray</a> for land, and five new towns were founded in Muscogee territory, among them Running Water, Nickajack, Long Island, Crow Town and Lookout Mountain; from then on the band was often called the Lower Cherokee. From Running Water Dragging Canoe led attacks across the Southeast, and his warriors raided as far as Indiana, Kentucky and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> alongside the Northwestern Confederacy, which they helped establish.</p><p>After Dragging Canoe's death in 1792 his chosen successor, John Watts, known as Young Tassel, moved the base to Willstown, nearer his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a> allies, and concluded a treaty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a> with the Spanish governor of West Florida, Arturo O'Neill de Tyrone, for arms and supplies. The war ended with the Treaty of Tellico Blockhouse in late 1794.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Former warriors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-creek">Lower Towns</a> then dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> affairs: the first three Principal Chiefs, Little Turkey, Black Fox and Pathkiller, had all fought under Dragging Canoe. Asked in 1799 what kind of people the Chickamauga were, the former warrior Richard Fields laughed and replied, &quot;They are Cherokee, and we know no difference.&quot; Later, Lower Towns leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sequoyah">Sequoyah</a> moved west to Arkansas Territory, founding what became the Cherokee Nation West.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chickamauga Cherokee” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
